<p>Lebanese and Israeli ambassadors were scheduled to meet in Washington on Tuesday for US-mediated talks, with direct negotiations between the two countries also on the table. Even before the meeting began, expectations for a breakthrough appeared low. Hezbollah leader Naim Qassem urged Lebanon to cancel the meeting, calling the talks pointless and repeating the group’s rejection of direct negotiations with Israel. Lebanese officials, however, said they planned to use the face-to-face talks to press for a ceasefire, while Hezbollah officials separately said the group would not be bound by any agreements that emerged from them. The dispute underscores how hard it remains to find a path away from war after Israeli strikes killed more than 2,000 people in Lebanon and displaced more than 1 million others, according to the reports.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/UG2YznK4XhS4zHZrT-5ItWzL1ZNhhlqkjtY20eaZ_9MulnoxPNsgV02kvnpjKscWiXyDq26fNue5iNGus2JvXMjzXTYwvLjhaoXHMvchaWQeiYJPwEZy5rmaaUzcYYUY99kYd9N16CCF9uMuF8aCflJYbJydj9DXc4kyOyhN3-4_Y8V-zfJIX6GjGmLVui2AtZV7LVSGgaNbQ04fcwhtiUBfMvBKG1Q4rHlQbTRUj0wWlbJL1FWqN3P8UuOGCMZgv3--6WTP8OKaJoFONQ' alt='Wafiq Safa, senior Hezbollah political council member, gestures as he speaks during an interview with The Associated Press in Beirut, Monday, April 13, 2026.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Weapons issue: Qassem said the Washington talks were a ploy to pressure Hezbollah to lay down its weapons, underscoring that the group sees the diplomacy as tied to its armed status, not just to border or ceasefire issues.</li><li>Street protests: Hezbollah supporters protested the planned talks and branded the Lebanese prime minister a &quot;zionist,&quot; showing the domestic political pressure around any engagement with Israel.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Naim Qassem: He urged the Lebanese government to scrap the Washington meeting and said Hezbollah would keep confronting Israeli attacks on Lebanon. (<a href='https://www.a